/*	Welcome to Terrains.json, world's best json object hash/keys since 0xD!

	Ultima V base is a TC basemod. Mod explorers should find interest in this mods' internals. [TC=total conversion, isBaseRuleset+TileSet+UnitSet]

	Base terrains are mostly the same as G&K, highlighting familiar tiles:
		Flatland =~ Plains+<features/Grass>, used primarily for Paths
		Surf: Usually found between Coast and Ocean as a water mid-body, originally subbed as Lakes
		+ Land&Water de-edging: cloned base tiles de-edge shorelines for decors, suffixed '[land] beach' and '[water] shore'
		+ Rough Ocean: Vis blocking ocean tiles, w/ specific buff/de-buffs
		? Lakes: May add specific Lake terrain for Lost Lake etc
		Tundra|Snow: Only one default instance atm, glass sword
	TerrainFeatures:
		Swamp =~ Marsh, basically the same
		+ Brush: light vegetation
		+ Woods =~ Forest
		Forest: Heavier density
		Thicket =~ Jungle, more apt naming for a European fantasy setting
		- Ice,Atoll
		? Flood plains, possibly useful for bridges/ as an underbody TF
	Finally TerrainDecor:
		+ bridges/
		+ paths/
		+ rivers/ - a mapdev trickplay, requiring placing actual river as guides then rivers/ decor to follow river flows
	Special assets:
		features/ - for ruleVariant terrain render layering, or other indirect tile render assets
		editor/ - useful map editor related sprites for better mapdev
	tOdO:
		> Add minimal base terrain tile coloring i.e. `"RGB": [99, 40, 7],`
		> Asset blocks are generally bare atm

	MIGRATION STRATEGY - retain original names as legacy, color tint them, seek/replace in editor.
		Far below in the `migration` section there's a smattering of various terrains that
		exist in the legacy Britannia map (Ultima-tileset mod). In moving to a basemod, many
		assets have been updated. Rather than rebuild the map from scratch, assets to be swapped
		have been re-colored to highlight map editor sprites that might be cloned sprites but
		need to be swapped for new assets. This way all legacy assets are accounted for when
		updating, otherwise it's too easy to miss identical looking terrains with new names.

